From 9d209af36fa4f1d5e4b77d2eae639a5262b20f5b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cpkt9762 <cpkt9762@163.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 01:06:14 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] fix(store): CJK search via trigram FTS with per-term fallback
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

FTS5 tables were created without an explicit tokenizer, so they used
unicode61. That tokenizer treats an unbroken run of Han characters as a
single token, which makes substring queries miss: searching 代理 never
matches a document holding 反向代理. Measured on a 500-row Chinese corpus,
recall against a LIKE ground truth was 13/47 for 代理 and 17/56 for 内存.

Switch observations_fts and prompts_fts to tokenize='trigram' and route
each query term by length:

  - Terms of three or more characters go to the trigram index.
  - Shorter terms cannot produce a trigram shingle, so they fall back to
    a bounded scan.

The routing is per term rather than all-or-nothing. A query mixing lengths
("反向代理 内存") is common in CJK, where two-character words sit beside
four-character ones. Deciding on "every term is short" would send such a
query down the FTS path and silently drop its short half, returning zero
rows where ten match.

The fallback itself splits by script. CJK has no word delimiters, so a
short CJK term is matched as a plain substring. A short Latin term matched
that way would be far too loose -- "go" hit 378 rows on the sample corpus
against 45 real word occurrences -- so those use GLOB character classes to
approximate word boundaries, preserving the pre-trigram behaviour for
queries like "v2".

Results on the same corpus, against a LIKE/GLOB ground truth over 20
queries spanning two-character CJK, four-character CJK, mixed-length CJK,
Latin, and a negative control: 7/20 correct before, 20/20 after.

Cost: the trigram index is roughly 6.8x the source text, and sub-trigram
terms scan rather than seek -- about 32ms across 18k rows versus 0.5ms for
an indexed term.

Relates to #140, #537.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
